      strategy: "Less about following footprints, more about 'spotting and stalking' based on predicted movement. The 'Spot and Stalk' Technique: Position on high ridge or koppie at first light. Using high-quality optics (10x42 binoculars and spotting scopes), meticulously scan opposite slopes and thickets. Kudu rarely seen standing in open - instead look for parts of animal: glint of sun on curve of horn, twitch of large ear, horizontal line of back amidst vertical trees. Once suitable bull located, stalk planned - involves closing distance (often from 1000 yards down to 100 yards) while paying strict attention to wind. Kudu have mediocre sense of sight but possessing hearing and smell that are legendary. Swirling wind in valley is Kudu hunter's worst enemy. When tracks show transition from gallop to trot, freeze and scan - bull likely stationary and watching. Ambush Hunting: In dry season, ambush from blind over waterhole or mineral lick can be effective - allows careful assessment of trophy quality as animals generally unaware of hunter's presence, particularly useful for bowhunters requiring close proximity (under 40 yards). Tracking: Tracking solitary bull through Kalahari sand or thick bushveld is art form - requires interpreting spoor to determine not just direction of travel, but animal's pace and intent.",
