      hornLength: "45-92 cm (18-36 in). Trophy quality often determined by final few inches. Look for tips that point straight up or slightly outwards. Tips that hook aggressively inward and toward each other often indicate horn has finished growing and is curving back on itself, potentially sacrificing length. Mature rams have heavy bases that appear to nearly touch on forehead. Ridges should be well-defined and carry well up horn. Smooth bases indicate youth, while worn, smooth tips indicate old age. SCI minimum: 52 inches (composite score of length of both horns plus circumference of bases). Rowland Ward minimum: 23 5/8 inches (measured along curve of longest horn). A 24-inch impala is considered 'Holy Grail' benchmark for most hunters.",
