      hornLength: "Both sexes carry horns, trait that complicates trophy selection. Horns rise from single, elevated bony pedicel on summit of skull, often covered in hair. Growth pattern complex and bracket-shaped: Rise - horns grow upwards and slightly forwards from pedicel. Curve - they then curve outwards. Return - finally, they hook sharply backwards, forming shape often likened to 'cowboy stance' or handlebars of motorcycle. In mature bulls, horn bases thick and knobby, often growing so close together that touch or fuse, leaving no gap on pedicel. In contrast, female horns more slender, lack heavy boss at base, and almost always show distinct gap between horns where exit skull. Male horns: 50-66 cm (20-26 in). Female horns: 45-55 cm (18-22 in). Trophy Judgment: Key Indicators for Trophy Bull - The Bases: Definitive check. Mature bull has thick, heavy horn bases. In trophy-class animal, bases swollen and nearly touching on pedicel. If clear gap of skin or hair between horn bases, likely cow or young bull. The 'Figure 7': When viewed from side, horn shape should resemble number '7' or backward question mark. In trophy bulls, tips of horns point straight back or slightly upwards. If tips hook downwards, trophy score lower. Body Mass: Bulls have visibly thicker neck and more muscular shoulder region. Coat often darkens to deep purple-brown on shoulders with age. Trophy Minimums: Safari Club International (SCI): 62 inches (sum of lengths of both horns + circumference of bases). Rowland Ward: 23 inches (length of longest horn along curve).",
