      shotPlacement: "Broadside: Lower third of body, directly behind front shoulder. Account for downward slope of spine (hindquarters higher than shoulders). Quartering-away: Enter behind ribs, travel forward into opposite shoulder through liver and lungs. Avoid head/neck shots - small target, risk of damaging trophy cape. Wounded lechwe in deep reed bed almost impossible to recover.",
      trophy: "Lyre-shaped horns sweeping backward then curving forward at tips. SCI minimum: 58 points (Method 7 - length + base circumference). Rowland Ward minimum: 26 inches (longest horn). Trophy class: 26+ inches (gold medal), exceptional 30+ inches. Look for thick base with heavy prominent rings extending at least halfway up, wide elegant 'S' curve, symmetrical horns. Mature rams: deep muscular chest, thick neck. In Black/Kafue subspecies, darkening coat and shoulder patches indicate age/trophy status.",
      meat: "Widely regarded as one of most delicious and healthy African game species. Exceptionally lean (3.2g fat/100g vs 10g+ in beef), extremely high protein (30%), rich in iron and zinc. Fine-grained, tender texture compared to high-quality veal or prime beef. Naturally 'sweeter' and less 'gamey' than savanna species due to diet of lush aquatic plants. Yields significant high-quality venison. Primary cuts: Loin/fillet (carpaccio, grilling), haunch (roasting, steaks), shoulder (potjiekos, biltong), shanks (stews). Favorite for biltong and droëwors - leanness prevents rancidity, natural flavor holds up to traditional spices.",
      difficulty: "High - Unique and challenging experience requiring departure from typical bushveld stalk. Open terrain with high visibility but scarce cover. Extremely vigilant with excellent eyesight - can spot human silhouette from miles away. Requires long circuitous loops, crawling through shallow water/mud, use of 'dead ground' and reed lines. Timing crucial - early morning/late afternoon best. Midday heat shimmer makes range-finding difficult. Specialized gear required: waders, waterfowl camouflage, tall shooting sticks. Aquatic escape makes recovery challenging.",
