      shotPlacement: "CRITICAL: Most common mistake is shooting too high. Pronounced shoulder hump and standing mane make back look higher than spine actually is. Thoracic spine dips significantly between shoulders. Shot placed 'high shoulder' often passes through dorsal spines above spinal cord - 'spinal shock' drops animal instantly but recovers seconds later and runs off. Broadside: Trace back of front leg up into body. Heart sits very low in 'V' formed by brisket and leg. Lungs sit above heart. Aim point: Vertical line of leg, one-third of way up body (NOT halfway up - that is too high). Quartering away: Ideal shot. Aim for off-side shoulder (far side leg). Bullet enters behind ribs, travels through liver and lungs, lodges in far shoulder anchoring animal. Frontal shot: AVOID - long sloping face, nose covers chest 'sticking point', bullet can deflect off snout or sternum, narrow target area protected by heavy bone. Reaction: Heart-shot executes 'death run' (50-100 yards) before collapsing. Wounded animal circles back downwind. If cornered, will charge - approach all downed animals from rear, poke eye with long reed/barrel to ensure corneal reflex absent before touching.",
